ed in that  fluid of the nucleus and so our  directions say free-floating nuclei  sites free-floating Adnan's and thymines  and cytosines and guanines match up so  look on the left strip of DNA I have a C  on top top left I have a C well I hope  you a G for guanine will flow down in  and match up the next one down is a T so  when a is going to float in the next one  down I have an A on the left so a T will  float in the next one down here comes a  see the next one down here comes a T the  next one down here comes a G well the  same thing happens to the DNA on the  right of the screen only replication  takes place in the opposite direction so  in this case we're going to start at the  bottom and work our way up there's a G  on the bottom here comes a C next next  one up there's a T on the right here  comes an A next one up here comes a G  next here is an adenine next here's a  thymine next here's a cytosine and so  what we've created here are two pieces  of DNA now we're not quite done but  that's kind of the main gist of it right  there but let's finish these up step 3  there's an enzyme and a different enzyme  by the name of DNA polymerase that helps  to reconnect the two strands and I'm  being a little simplistic here by in  light by illustrating DNA polymerase as  a bottle of glue and it helps to glue  and bond the add means the thymines and  the guanine cytosine is all together and  so there's this enzyme called DNA  polymerase that helps to reconnect and  REE bond and basically zip up the DNA  and complete this process and when the  process is finished I hope you notice  both strands of DNA are identical look  at the the letter code on the top c and  g to the left on the right cng next  letters down TNA next letters down TNA  next letters down a NT and then a NT the  identical the the DNA strands are  identical to one another well you know  in the creation of all these a's and t's  and c's and g's remember there's 3  billion 3 billion pairs of a's and t's  and 